azfilesauth (1.0-14) unstable; urgency=medium

  * feat: per-identity Kerberos credential caches (krb5cc_<cruid>) so multiple
    distinct identities (managed identity, workload identity, direct OAuth token)
    can mount the same storage account on the same node, each with its own
    credential cache instead of the single node-wide krb5cc_0.
  * azfilesauthmanager gains an optional `--cache-uid <uid>` argument; the native
    functions extern_smb_set_credential_oauth_token and extern_smb_list_credential
    gain a trailing `unsigned int cache_uid` parameter. ABI CHANGE for direct
    C/ctypes callers only, which must pass the new argument (0 keeps the previous
    behavior). CLI users, including azurefile-csi-driver, are unaffected: the
    bundled azfilesauthmanager is updated in this package.
  * Per-identity caches are chowned to the owning uid and chmod 0600; for a
    per-identity cache a chown/chmod failure returns an error (the kernel
    cifs.upcall reads the cache as the cruid, so an un-owned cache is unusable).
  * azfilesrefresh renews each per-identity cache in place and keys active mounts
    by (endpoint, cruid), canonicalizing privatelink endpoints.
  * feat: provision the per-identity passwd entry natively. For a non-zero cruid
    that has no passwd entry, extern_smb_set_credential_oauth_token now writes an
    inert stand-in entry (nologin shell, /nonexistent home) before dropping euid,
    so cifs.upcall's getpwuid(cruid) resolves when it reads krb5cc_<cruid>. The
    passwd file is coordinated with useradd/usermod via an fcntl lock on
    <dir>/.pwd.lock (lckpwdf-compatible) and selectable with the new PASSWD_FILE
    config key (default /etc/passwd; set to /host/etc/passwd when run in the
    driver container so the host NSS that cifs.upcall consults is updated).

 -- Roger Nhan <rogernhan@microsoft.com>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:00:00 +0000

